WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.720
Good morning John. In 1911, in a very early science fiction novel, Hugo Gernsback became the first person to predict

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:14.250
personalized news, when he wrote,  "the morning newspapers were transmitted to the sleeping subscribers by wire at about 5:00 a.m.

00:00:14.589 --> 00:00:21.358
The newspaper office, notified by each subscriber what kind of news is desirable, furnished only such news"

00:00:21.939 --> 00:00:28.109
More than a hundred years later, that future is more than now. Our personalised news sources would more accurately be called

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:36.090
Information and entertainment feeds and they contain everything from pictures of your cousin's baby to the unfiltered thoughts of the President of the United States.

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:38.370
But in Gernsback future

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:43.820
it was you who decided what news you wanted to see  - and that idea on its own may be troubling.

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:49.440
But now who decides what ends up in your feed isn't you. It isn't even a person.

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:55.260
It's a computer program and increasingly a program that built itself in ways that no one fully

00:00:55.539 --> 00:00:59.939
understands;  to satisfy not /your/ needs, but the needs of the /platform/.

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:08.010
These machines are given desired outcomes, but how they get there is up to them and their goals are tightly held industry secrets.

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:12.740
But here are two things that are very clear. One: the information in your feed

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:16.120
And how it is served to you, can have a lasting effect on who you are

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:23.580
And how you feel, and two: the business model of social media relies on selling that power for a profit.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:26.430
Due to a failing in Facebook policy and enforcement

00:01:26.979 --> 00:01:33.868
Cambridge Analytica was able to create psychological profiles of tens of millions of Facebook users. "We have somewhere close to four or five

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:38.699
thousand data points on every adult in the United States". And then they could create not just

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:47.189
advertisements, but news stories and even entire news outlets specifically to amplify and exploit the fears and anxieties of

00:01:47.189 --> 00:01:53.129
voters. This was a violation of Facebook's rules, but not of any laws because in the U.S.

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:59.490
there are so few laws regulating these companies. In fact, Facebook doesn't want anyone else to have your data

00:01:59.799 --> 00:02:02.549
Because your data is their most valuable asset

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:08.190
But as artificial intelligence gets better, even small amounts of data will likely be enough

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:16.409
smaller and smaller signals are becoming clearer and clearer. Machines are now able to tell with 91% accuracy what a man's sexual orientation is

00:02:16.629 --> 00:02:21.298
from profile photos. If you're wondering why Facebook is worth half a trillion dollars

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:26.909
It's because of its ability to capture your attention and because of what it knows about you.

00:02:27.189 --> 00:02:31.919
When fed enough data, machines can determine shocking detail with tiny amounts of input

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:37.619
And they will use what they know about you, your worldview, and even your emotional state to help others

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:43.950
manipulate you -  as long as those people have the money to spend. This is not a secret conspiracy,

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:50.940
it's their business model. Facebook isn't the product that's being sold - Facebook is free. You are not the customer,

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:54.119
you are the /product/. On the social internet, your attention

00:02:54.250 --> 00:03:01.889
is their dollars. And for them that means that anything that gains attention is good and whether they decide to have any

00:03:01.959 --> 00:03:08.009
regard for truth, for the stability of society, for the health of their users is at the moment

00:03:08.799 --> 00:03:15.029
entirely up to them. And while some of us will dive into these issues to at least understand, most people

00:03:15.489 --> 00:03:21.869
won't have the time to do that and ultimately we /need/ Facebook. It's socially expected,

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:24.000
it's necessary, and it's a useful platform.

00:03:24.549 --> 00:03:31.469
We're trapped, so the only thing to do is remember and to help others learn if you don't control the feed,

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:33.190
the feed

00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:35.190
Is controlling you.

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:37.230
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:42.359
John, our new media literacy series on Crash Course with Jay Smooth is so good and

00:03:42.669 --> 00:03:48.359
fascinating, and I want to tell anyone who goes to watch it that it's gonna change the way you see the world around you forever.

00:03:48.519 --> 00:03:53.369
It might be a little less cut and dried, but it's gonna be a lot more accurate.

